CAT = "casfaRole"; SEG = "ROL"
Q = [
("Before you touch a conscious adult who needs first aid, what must you get?",
 "Their consent", ["A witness present", "Their health card", "A supervisor's approval"],
 "A conscious adult has the right to accept or refuse care, so you identify yourself, explain what you plan to do and ask permission first."),
("An adult is unresponsive and alone. What kind of consent lets you give first aid?",
 "Implied consent", ["Written consent", "Informed consent", "Delegated consent"],
 "The law assumes an unresponsive person would want help, which is called implied consent. You do not need to wait for family or a form."),
("A conscious 6-year-old is hurt and a parent is nearby. Whose consent do you ask for?",
 "The parent's or guardian's", ["The child's own consent", "No one's, since it is urgent", "The oldest bystander's"],
 "A child cannot legally give consent, so you ask the parent or guardian if they are present. If no parent or guardian is there, consent is implied."),
("A conscious adult clearly refuses your help. What should you do?",
 "Respect it; call EMS if needed", ["Give care anyway, gently", "Leave the scene at once", "Wait until they faint, then treat"],
 "You cannot treat a competent adult who refuses, but you can stay nearby, call EMS and keep trying to persuade them. Note who witnessed the refusal."),
("Once you have started first aid, when may you stop?",
 "When someone as trained takes over", ["When you feel the person looks fine", "When a bystander tells you to", "After 10 minutes of first aid"],
 "Stopping without handing over to someone with equal or higher training can be abandonment. You may also stop if the scene becomes unsafe or you are too exhausted to go on."),
("What do Good Samaritan laws in most of Canada generally protect?",
 "A rescuer acting in good faith", ["Any act done at the scene", "Paid rescuers on duty only", "Rescuers who refuse to help"],
 "Provincial and territorial Good Samaritan laws protect people who help in good faith and without gross negligence. The exact wording varies by province and territory."),
("Which province's charter places a legal duty on everyone to help a person whose life is in peril?",
 "Quebec", ["Ontario", "Alberta", "British Columbia"],
 "Quebec's Charter of human rights and freedoms requires everyone to help a person whose life is in peril, personally or by calling for help, unless it would put them or someone else in danger or they have another valid reason. Other provinces rely on voluntary Good Samaritan protection."),
("What is the first thing to do when you arrive at an emergency?",
 "Check the scene for danger", ["Check the person's breathing", "Start chest compressions", "Look for a medical ID"],
 "You cannot help anyone if you become a casualty yourself. Check for hazards such as traffic, fire, electricity or fumes before you approach."),
("A person lies on a busy road with traffic still moving. What should you do first?",
 "Make the scene safe if you can", ["Move the person straight away", "Start CPR where they lie", "Wait in your car for police"],
 "Stop or warn traffic only if it is safe, and use hazard lights or bystanders. Move a casualty only when there is an immediate danger you cannot control."),
("Which item best protects you from blood and body fluids while giving care?",
 "Disposable gloves", ["Cotton work gloves", "A paper face mask", "Safety glasses alone"],
 "Disposable nitrile or vinyl gloves form a barrier against blood. Cotton gloves soak fluids through, and a paper mask does not protect your hands."),
("How should you take off used disposable gloves?",
 "Without touching their outside", ["By pulling at the fingertips", "After rinsing them in water", "By snapping them off quickly"],
 "Pinch one glove at the wrist and peel it off inside out, then slide a clean finger under the other. Wash your hands afterwards."),
("How long should you scrub with soap when washing your hands after giving care?",
 "At least 20 seconds", ["About 5 seconds", "At least 2 minutes", "About 10 seconds"],
 "Public Health Agency of Canada advice is to wash with soap and water for at least 20 seconds. Use an alcohol-based sanitizer if soap and water are not available."),
("What should you use when giving rescue breaths to a stranger?",
 "A breathing barrier", ["A clean cloth only", "A plastic grocery bag", "No barrier at all"],
 "A pocket mask or face shield with a one-way valve lowers the risk of infection. A cloth or bag gives no real protection and can block airflow."),
("In the primary assessment, what is the first thing you check on the person?",
 "Responsiveness", ["Skin colour", "Pupil size", "Medical history"],
 "Tap the shoulder and shout to see if the person responds. An unresponsive person needs EMS called and a breathing check right away."),
("How long should you take to check an unresponsive person for normal breathing?",
 "Up to 10 seconds", ["About 30 seconds", "A full minute", "At least 20 seconds"],
 "Look at the chest and listen for no more than 10 seconds. A longer check delays CPR, and gasping does not count as normal breathing."),
("Which problem do you look for during the primary assessment, along with breathing?",
 "Severe bleeding", ["A sprained ankle", "A minor bruise", "Mild nausea"],
 "The primary assessment finds threats to life: unresponsiveness, airway and breathing problems, and severe bleeding. Minor injuries wait for the secondary assessment."),
("An injured adult can talk and breathes normally. When do you do a head-to-toe check?",
 "After the primary assessment", ["Before checking the scene", "Only if EMS asks you to", "Before you get consent"],
 "Once you know there is no immediate threat to life, a secondary assessment looks for other injuries and gathers history. You still need the person's consent."),
("What does the S stand for in the SAMPLE history?",
 "Signs and symptoms", ["Skin and sweating", "Sleep and stress", "Size and shape"],
 "SAMPLE stands for signs and symptoms, allergies, medications, past medical history, last oral intake and events leading up to the incident."),
("When you call 9-1-1, what should you do before hanging up?",
 "Wait until the dispatcher says to", ["Give your name, then hang up", "Hang up once help is on its way", "Hang up after giving the address"],
 "The dispatcher may need more details or may coach you through CPR. Stay on the line until they tell you to end the call."),
("You are alone with an unresponsive adult and have a phone. What should you do?",
 "Call 9-1-1 on speaker and stay", ["Run to find a landline first", "Text a friend for help", "Give CPR, then call in 5 minutes"],
 "Putting the phone on speaker lets you call EMS and start care at once. The dispatcher can guide you while you work."),
("A bystander offers to help at an emergency. What is the best use of them?",
 "Send them to call EMS and get an AED", ["Ask them to wait across the road", "Have them search the victim's bag", "Ask them to film the scene"],
 "Giving a bystander a clear job speeds up help. Point at them and tell them exactly what to do and to come back to you."),
("When should you move an injured person before EMS arrives?",
 "When the scene is dangerous", ["When they look uncomfortable", "To get them out of the rain", "Whenever a crowd gathers"],
 "Moving someone can worsen an injury, especially to the spine. Move them only when staying puts them or you in immediate danger."),
("An unresponsive adult is breathing normally and has no suspected spinal injury. How should you position them?",
 "In the recovery position", ["Flat on their back", "Sitting up against a wall", "Face down on the ground"],
 "The recovery position keeps the airway open and lets fluids drain from the mouth. Keep checking their breathing until EMS arrives."),
("What is the purpose of the recovery position?",
 "To keep the airway clear", ["To raise the blood pressure", "To keep the legs warm", "To steady the heart rate"],
 "Rolling the person onto their side lets vomit or saliva drain out and stops the tongue from blocking the throat."),
("Which finding should make you suspect a head, neck or spinal injury?",
 "A fall from a height", ["A small cut on the hand", "A nosebleed from heat", "A bee sting on the arm"],
 "Falls from a height, diving injuries, vehicle crashes and blows to the head all suggest a possible spinal injury. Keep the person still."),
("Why should you write down what happened and the care you gave?",
 "To hand accurate details to EMS", ["To file it with the police", "To prove the person was at fault", "To send it to the person's insurer"],
 "Notes on times, findings and care help EMS and doctors. Many workplaces also require a first aid record; the rules vary by province and territory."),
("Who sets how many first aiders and kits a workplace must have?",
 "Provincial or federal OHS rules", ["Each first aid training agency", "Each employer as it sees fit", "Each worker for themselves"],
 "Workplace first aid requirements come from the occupational health and safety rules of each province, territory or federal jurisdiction, so they vary across Canada."),
("A person who was helped looks pale, upset and keeps asking what happened. What helps most?",
 "Calm reassurance", ["A drink of strong coffee", "Leaving them on their own", "Firm instructions to relax"],
 "Staying with the person, speaking calmly and explaining what is happening reduces fear. Fear and anxiety can make some conditions worse."),
]
